    "description": "Precision agriculture uses information about differences within fields and across time to guide farm management. Iranian research on soil moisture demonstrates relevant technical capabilities, while leaving nationwide adoption and farm-level benefits to be established separately.",
    "tags": ["Precision agriculture", "Soil moisture", "Irrigation", "Sentinel-1", "Farm management"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T06:51:30Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T06:51:30Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Precision Agriculture in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover how field observations and satellite estimates can inform Iranian farm management, and why technical accuracy differs from proven farm benefits."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "management-principle",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Responding to differences within a farm",
        "content": "Precision agriculture begins with a management question: should different parts of a field receive the same treatment? The International Society of Precision Agriculture defines the approach around collecting and interpreting information about variation across space and time, then using it to support decisions. Its purpose includes improved resource use, productivity, quality, profitability, and sustainability. These are objectives to evaluate, rather than benefits guaranteed by owning particular equipment. [s1]\n\nThe distinction matters when reading about agricultural technology in Iran. A satellite image or prediction model supplies information; precision management also requires deciding what that information means for a particular crop and operation. A technically impressive map is therefore one component of a management strategy, rather than evidence that the strategy has already delivered better outcomes. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iranian-soil-moisture-study",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An Iranian example: estimating soil moisture in wheat",
        "content": "Research published in 2026 examined irrigated winter-wheat fields on the Qorveh–Dehgolan Plain in western Iran. The study combined field measurements with radar observations from Sentinel-1, a satellite mission, to investigate whether machine-learning methods could estimate soil moisture. Field observations were collected at georeferenced locations on several dates during the 2022 growing season, aligned with satellite overpasses. [s2]\n\nThe abstract reports that boosting and random-forest methods performed better than the other tested algorithms, but accuracy declined as vegetation cover increased and crops developed. This is a useful limitation: a method's performance can change during the growing season, even within the crop and area for which it was assessed. [s2]\n\nThe result supports a specific capability—estimating soil moisture under the study's conditions. It does not by itself demonstrate reduced pumping, increased yield, or a profitable commercial service. Those outcomes require evidence beyond the prediction performance reported in the abstract. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "management-zones",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Turning observations into management zones",
        "content": "A useful general framework is to combine what farmers see, what they measure, and what they harvest. Ontario's agricultural guidance describes management zones built from information such as topography, soil characteristics, and crop yield. Geographic information systems bring these layers together with image interpretation and grower knowledge to develop and validate recommendations. This is an explanatory framework, not evidence that the same system is widely deployed in Iran. [s3]\n\nFor an Iranian application, the framework helps distinguish stages of progress. Detecting a moisture pattern is an observation task. Establishing that the pattern warrants a different intervention is an interpretation task. Implementing that intervention and checking the result is a management task. Keeping these stages separate makes research claims easier to assess. [s1][s3]\n\nPrecision agriculture is consequently best understood as a way to make decisions more responsive to local conditions. The Iranian wheat study demonstrates a relevant research pathway; evidence about sustained use, costs, and environmental results must still be assessed at the scale of the farms and regions concerned. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "decision-workflow",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "From field variation to a management decision",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Field and soil observations\"] --> C[\"Combine information by location\"]\n  B[\"Images and yield records\"] --> C\n  C --> D[\"Interpret differences with grower knowledge\"]\n  D --> E[\"Develop management zones\"]\n  E --> F[\"Prepare and validate input recommendations\"]\n```",
        "summary": "This general workflow explains how several kinds of information can support management zones. It does not describe a verified nationwide Iranian service or claim that satellite observations automatically control farm equipment.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      }
    ],
